    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d247">

                <pb facs="0331" n="247" sameAs="frus:frus1918Supp02/pg247"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72111/4971</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Assistant Secretary of Labor</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Post</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 54274/8–B</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1917-05-02">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">May 2, 1917</hi></date>. <lb/> [<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Received May 4.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Alluding to your letter of the 20th
                        ultimo,<anchor n="1" xml:id="d247-f1"/> with which you transmitted copy of a
                    petition addressed to the Swedish Minister by a committee of the officers and
                    crew of the Austrian steamers <hi rendition="#italic">Martha Washington, Dora,
                        Ida</hi>, and <hi rendition="#italic">Himalaia</hi>,<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d247-f2"/> I beg to state (merely for the purpose of completing your
                    record, the fact, of course, already being known to you), that as soon as it
                    became apparent that Austrians were not likely to become alien enemies, in the
                    immediate future at any rate, instructions were given by this Department to
                    handle the cases of all officers and crewmen taken from Austrian vessels simply
                    under the provisions of the immigration law. This course has been followed and
                    as a result all those who could pass the immigration examination, comprising the
                    vast majority of those taken into custody, have been released and admitted to
                    the country.</p>
                <p>Respectfully,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Louis F. Post</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
